allout over the nuclear innovation campus deal could narrow the margin, but a genuinely competitive race would require a historic shift.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Brad Little (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Idaho's governor's race pits Republican incumbent Brad Little, seeking a third term, against Democratic nominee Terri Pickens, a small business owner. Independent John Stegner, Libertarian Paul Sand, and Constitution Party candidate Pro-Life are also on the ballot. Little cleared his primary in May without serious difficulty, and the state's R+18 partisan lean anchors the race firmly in Republican territory.\n\nEvery major race rater calls the contest Solid or Safe Republican, and prediction markets on Kalshi and Polymarket price Little's chances near 97 percent. Polling is sparse: a September registered-voter survey showed Little ahead by ten points with a large share unallocated, while a July likely-voter poll had him up twenty-one. Pickens has campaigned on abortion rights alongside a statewide ballot initiative, and both she and Stegner have criticized Little's nuclear innovation campus deal, but none of it has dented the incumbent's standing.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0111, "margin": -14.27}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0115, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0087, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0158, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0168, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0122, "margin": -14.0}]}